      "message": {
       "content": "# Research Agenda: Reducing Hallucinated Citations in Language-Model Outputs\n\n## Framing\n\n\"Hallucinated citation\" is not one failure. Before proposing fixes, we need to separate at least four distinct error classes:\n\n| Class | Description |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **C1 \u2013 Fabrication** | The reference does not exist in any index (Crossref, Semantic Scholar, arXiv, PubMed). |\n| **C2 \u2013 Metadata corruption",
       "name": null,
       "reasoning": "The user wants a research agenda for reducing hallucinated citations in LLM outputs. They want 3-5 concrete experiments, each with:\n- Hypotheses\n- Datasets or how to build them\n- Metrics\n- Expected outcomes\n- What each result would change about the next step\n\nLet me think carefully about what \"hallucinated citations\" means and what a rigorous research agenda would look like. Hallucinated citations are references that the model generates that don't exist, have wrong authors, wrong years, wrong titles, wrong DOIs, or that exist but don't actually support the claim being made.\n\nLet me think about the key dimensions of this problem:\n1. The model \"remembers\" a citation that doesn't exist (fabrication)\n2. The model \"remembers\" a real citation but attributes the wrong claim to it (misattribution)\n3. The model \"remembers\" a real citation with correct metadata but the paper doesn't actually contain the cited claim (content mismatch)\n4. The model confuses two real papers (mixing metadata)\n5. The model generates plausible-sounding but non-existent citations (the most common and most harmful)\n\nA good research agenda should be structured so that each experiment builds on the previous one, and the results of one inform the design of the next. Let me think about what experiments would be most informative and concrete.\n\nExperiment 1: Characterization / Baseline - What's the actual rate and taxonomy of hallucinated citations?\n- This is foundational. We need to know what we're dealing with before we can fix it.\n- Hypothesis: Hallucinated citations cluster in specific domains, and the failure mode is primarily \"plausible fabrication\" rather than \"misattribution of real papers.\"\n- Dataset: Build a benchmark of prompts that elicit citations, then verify each citation against real databases (Crossref, Semantic Scholar, arXiv).\n- Metrics: Rate of non-existent citations",
       "reasoning_content": "The user wants a research agenda for reducing hallucinated citations in LLM outputs. They want 3-5 concrete experiments, each with:\n- Hypotheses\n- Datasets or how to build them\n- Metrics\n- Expected outcomes\n- What each result would change about the next step\n\nLet me think carefully about what \"hallucinated citations\" means and what a rigorous research agenda would look like. Hallucinated citations are references that the model generates that don't exist, have wrong authors, wrong years, wrong titles, wrong DOIs, or that exist but don't actually support the claim being made.\n\nLet me think about the key dimensions of this problem:\n1. The model \"remembers\" a citation that doesn't exist (fabrication)\n2. The model \"remembers\" a real citation but attributes the wrong claim to it (misattribution)\n3. The model \"remembers\" a real citation with correct metadata but the paper doesn't actually contain the cited claim (content mismatch)\n4. The model confuses two real papers (mixing metadata)\n5. The model generates plausible-sounding but non-existent citations (the most common and most harmful)\n\nA good research agenda should be structured so that each experiment builds on the previous one, and the results of one inform the design of the next. Let me think about what experiments would be most informative and concrete.\n\nExperiment 1: Characterization / Baseline - What's the actual rate and taxonomy of hallucinated citations?\n- This is foundational. We need to know what we're dealing with before we can fix it.\n- Hypothesis: Hallucinated citations cluster in specific domains, and the failure mode is primarily \"plausible fabrication\" rather than \"misattribution of real papers.\"\n- Dataset: Build a benchmark of prompts that elicit citations, then verify each citation against real databases (Crossref, Semantic Scholar, arXiv).\n- Metrics: Rate of non-existent citations",
